comments.body:
I took some time to investigate what would be required to move the loading of `dxil.dll` out of `dxcompiler.dll`'s `DllMain`. What I found is that [this commit](https://github.com/microsoft/DirectXShaderCompiler/commit/5a916c56d9244ce51512655f88ccbbc827f8b11d) is where it was moved to `DllMain`. Before that, dxil.dll would be loaded on-demand from `DxcCompiler::Compile`, and then unloaded after the function returned. The commit comment includes:

```
- Resolve lifetime of dxil.dll on dxcompiler.dll to guarantee access of memory generated from dxil.dll
```

Looking at the original code, I assume what this is referring to is the fact that when validation is enabled, the validator returns a "validated blob", and this is what's returned to the caller:

```cpp
              IFT(pValidator->Validate(
                pOutputBlob, DxcValidatorFlags_InPlaceEdit, &pValResult));
...
            CComPtr<IDxcBlob> pValidatedBlob;
            IFT(pValResult->GetResult(&pValidatedBlob));
            if (pValidatedBlob != nullptr) {
              std::swap(pOutputBlob, pValidatedBlob);
            }
            pValidator.Release();
```

So the memory for `pValidatedBlob` is allocated by `dxil.dll`, but when this function returns, `dxil.dll` may be unloaded (if it's the last reference to it), making this a dangling reference.

Thus, whatever change is made, dxil.dll must be loaded before compiling via `dxcompiler.dll`, and must remain loaded after the resulting blob is returned. One way to do this would be to tie the loading of dxil.dll to when `DxcCreateInstance` is called to create the compiler instance, and to unload `dxil.dll` when this compiler instance is destroyed, which I think could be done by doing the load/unload of `dxil.dll` in the ctor/dtor of the `DxcCompiler` class.

There are a few more things to look at, such as the fact that there are actually 3 DLLs that load `dxil.dll`: `dxcompiler`, `dxilconv`, and `dxrfallbackcompiller`. Also, I think this would not handle the case where the user uses `DxcCreateInstance` to create a validator object.

To summarize, I think this is a non-trivial change, and would require extensive testing. I'm not sure how well tested all the code paths related to the validator are presently.

Having said all that, I was thinking of a potential workaround for this loader lock problem. What if the client code makes sure to explicitly load `dxil.dll` before loading `dxcompiler.dll`? I think this should resolve any deadlock situation that may occur because `dxcompiler.dll`'s loading of `dxil.dll` would simply increase a ref count and not perform any actual loading. Does this make sense? If so, this is probably what we'll do in the short-term in Chrome.
